// The `text` namespace's three contract operations (area 19, "Every
// standard type meets one contract"): each one operation over every
// standard type, as `json.read` is one over every input type, listed in
// `jadpo-std`'s operation table and run by `jadpo-value`'s `standard`
// module over each type's data entry. The rest of `text` comes with
// chunk 5.5f.
//
// `T` in `format` and `from` is a standard type, or a type built on one;
// which fit, and how each is shown or written, is the type's data entry,
// which the checker reads. `zone:` and `format:` mean something only for
// the types whose entry takes them (the time types), and those need them.
// `as:` in `parse` is a type written out, a standard type or a type built
// on one or on `Text`, `Int`, `Decimal` or `Bool`, and the result is that
// type: text that isn't one at all fails `malformed`, and the type's own
// rules fail by their names, as a construction's do.

public library text {
    /// `value` shown to a person in `locale`, in the type's own words.
    function format(value: T, locale: Locale, zone: Zone?, format: TimeFormat?) -> PresentationText {}

    /// `value`'s canonical text, the form `parse` reads back.
    function from(value: T) -> Text {}

    /// The value of type `as` that `raw`, its canonical text, holds.
    function parse(raw: Text, as: Type) -> T {}
}
